# Agent Workflow

## Purpose

These rules define how AI coding agents must work inside the repository.

The goal is to keep changes small, intentional, reviewable, and aligned with the project architecture.

---

## Core Workflow

- Implement changes in small, reviewable batches.
- Keep changes minimal and targeted.
- Do not modify unrelated files.
- Do not perform opportunistic refactoring while solving a targeted task.
- Follow the project map before creating files, folders, imports, tags, scripts, or abstractions.
- Place new specs in `tests/<layer>/<feature>/`, never at the `tests/ui/` or `tests/api/` root.
- Do not invent architecture when an existing convention exists.
- Do not overengineer abstractions.

---

## Default Implementation Cycle

For UI feature work, follow the dedicated Feature Lifecycle rule.

Default cycle:

```text
plan -> data -> Page Objects/components -> tests -> verify -> cleanup temporary debug artifacts -> heal -> harden
```

Rule reference for cleanup:

- `.cursor/rules/temporary-debug-artifact-cleanup.rules.mdc`

Do not go directly into test implementation when data, structure, or existing abstractions are not understood.

For non-UI work, adapt the cycle to the relevant architecture rules.

Examples:

```text
API:
plan -> payload/data -> client/request -> tests -> verify -> heal -> harden
```

```text
Framework utility:
plan -> existing usage check -> minimal utility -> targeted tests/checks -> verify
```

---

## Planning

Before implementing, identify:

- requested change;
- affected layer;
- existing relevant files;
- reusable project abstractions;
- existing helpers under `src/test/assertions/**`, `src/test/reporting/**`, and related project layers;
- required data;
- required verification command.

Before adding helper logic to specs, search existing project helpers and reuse or extend them.

Rule reference:

- `.cursor/rules/spec-helper-policy.rules.mdc`

For large changes or refactors, first explain:

- scope;
- affected layers;
- risks;
- proposed migration path;
- verification plan.

Do not start large refactors without review.

---

## Abstraction Defaults

Prefer the simplest correct abstraction.

### UI work

- For UI work, default to Page Object first.
- Add Component only after clear reuse or real UI complexity appears.
- Keep Components UI-focused.
- Do not create domain flows for simple page or component behavior.

### API work

- For API work, default to request/response model.
- Add thin API client only when endpoints are reused or grouping improves clarity.
- Do not introduce Page Objects, Components, or UI patterns for API tests.
- Do not create complex service hierarchies.

### Utilities

- Add utility only when pure reusable logic appears.
- Do not create utility modules for one-off logic.
- Keep utilities domain-neutral if they live in framework core.

### Fixtures

- Add fixture only when reusable dependency wiring, setup, or repeated object creation appears.
- Do not create fixtures for one-off values.
- Do not hide the action under test inside fixtures.
- Keep fixtures thin and focused on setup/wiring.

---

## Reuse Before Create

Before adding a new abstraction, check whether an existing one can be reused or extended.

Check existing:

- Page Objects;
- Components;
- fixtures;
- builders;
- generators;
- clients;
- utilities;
- rules;
- project map entries.

Do not create duplicate abstractions with overlapping responsibilities.

---

## Verification

After code changes:

1. Run impacted spec(s) or targeted checks.
2. Run relevant type/lint checks when applicable.
3. Run the repository quality gate command defined by the project map.

Do not mark work complete if verification was skipped.

Before marking work complete, remove temporary discovery/debug artifacts created during the task.

Rule reference:

- `.cursor/rules/temporary-debug-artifact-cleanup.rules.mdc`

If verification cannot be run, state clearly:

- what was changed;
- what should be run;
- why verification was not executed.

---

## Healing

When tests fail, fix the root cause.

Do not hide failures by:

- adding `waitForTimeout`;
- blindly increasing timeouts;
- weakening assertions;
- adding fake assertions;
- changing expected behavior without requirement confirmation;
- moving the action under test into hooks or fixtures.

---

## Hardening

Harden only reusable learnings.

Update rules, skills, utilities, or documentation only when the issue represents:

- a recurring pattern;
- an architectural risk;
- a convention gap;
- a reusable improvement.

Do not add new rules for one-off cases.

---

## Blockers

If required information is missing:

- do not invent paths, commands, tags, roles, users, endpoints, or architecture;
- inspect existing project conventions when possible;
- use the project map as the source of truth;
- ask for clarification only when the decision cannot be made safely.

---

## Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- large unreviewed refactors;
- unrelated file changes;
- creating abstractions before need appears;
- copying examples from rules as mandatory implementation;
- bypassing the project map;
- adding fixtures for one-off values;
- adding utilities for one-off logic;
- applying UI patterns to API tests;
- skipping verification;
- masking test failures instead of healing root causes;
- leaving temporary debug scripts, discovery JSON dumps, or scratch investigation files in the repository after implementation or healing.

---

## Main Principle

The agent must behave like a careful maintainer.

Small change.

Known layer.

Existing convention first.

Minimal abstraction.

Targeted verification.

Root-cause healing.

Cleanup temporary debug artifacts before finishing.

Reusable hardening only when justified.